Experiment 8: Observing Process Memory Maps Using pmap
A comprehensive technical manual examining process virtual address space layout, page permissions, resident memory, dirty pages, shared libraries, and heap allocation through deep inspection with pmap -x.
Experiment 8: Observing Process Memory Maps Using pmap
1. Aim
To examine the memory mappings of the CPU-bound and I/O-bound programs from Experiment 7 using the pmap command.
2. Procedure
Step 1: Run both programs
./io_bound &
./cpu_bound &


Step 2: Find their PIDs
pgrep -af 'io_bound|cpu_bound'


Step 3: Display the CPU-bound process memory map
pmap <CPU_PID>


For detailed output:
pmap -x <CPU_PID>


Step 4: Display the I/O-bound process memory map
pmap <IO_PID>


For detailed output:
pmap -x <IO_PID>


Replace the placeholders with the actual PIDs.
3. What to Expect in pmap Output
The two programs contain different instructions, but both are C programs running under Linux. Their memory maps therefore contain broadly similar types of regions.
Memory Region
Meaning in These Programs
Executable code
Machine instructions generated from the C source
Data/BSS
Storage for applicable global or static variables
Stack
Function-call information and local variables, where stored on the stack
Heap
Dynamic memory allocations, if any
Shared libraries
Libraries required by the program, such as the C standard library
Other mappings
Additional regions used by the runtime and operating system


In the CPU-bound program, counter is a local variable. In the I/O-bound program, fp is a local file pointer. Both programs may have stack mappings, but the compiler can choose how individual variables are stored.
Neither program explicitly calls malloc(), so neither needs a large dynamic allocation for its main task.
4. Understanding the Output
Run:
pmap -x <PID>


The output of pmap -x has six columns. Each row describes one contiguous region of the process's virtual address space.
4.1 Column Summary
Column
Simple Meaning
Address
Where the memory region starts
Kbytes
Total size of the region, in kilobytes
RSS
How much of the region is actually in RAM right now
Dirty
How much of the region has been changed
Mode
What the program is allowed to do with the region
Mapping
What the region belongs to


4.2 Each Column in Simple Words
Imagine a street with many plots of land. Each plot is one memory region of the process. The columns describe each plot.
Address: the house number
This is the number that tells you where the region starts. It is a virtual address, which means it is private to the process. Two different processes can have the same address and still use completely different memory.
Kbytes: the size of the plot
This is the total size reserved for the region. The program may not be using all of it yet, so a big number here does not mean a lot of RAM is being used.
RSS: the part that is actually occupied
RSS shows how much of the region is really loaded in physical RAM at this moment. Memory that has never been used is not counted. So RSS is always the same as or smaller than Kbytes.
Dirty: the part that has been changed
Dirty shows how much of the region the program has modified. Code is never changed, so its Dirty value is usually 0. Data and stack are changed while the program runs, so they show a Dirty value above 0. Dirty is always the same as or smaller than RSS.
Mode: the rules for the region
Mode tells you what the program may do with the region.
Letter
Meaning
Example
r
Read: the program can look at the memory
Almost every region
w
Write: the program can change the memory
Data and stack
x
Execute: the CPU can run the memory as instructions
Program code
-
This permission is not given
Any position


The first three letters are read, write and execute, in that order. For example:
r-x means the region can be read and run, but not changed. This is program code.
rw- means the region can be read and changed, but not run. This is data or stack.
r-- means the region can only be read, such as fixed constant values.
These rules protect the program from mistakes, such as accidentally overwriting its own code.
Mapping: the owner's name
This tells you what the region belongs to. It can be:
the program itself, such as cpu_bound
a shared library, such as libc.so.6
a special region, such as [ stack ] or [ anon ] (memory that is not tied to any file)
The exact names can differ between Linux systems.
4.3 Quick Summary
Size order: Dirty ≤ RSS ≤ Kbytes. The changed part sits inside the part in RAM, which sits inside the total size.
Mode tells you the type of region: r-x is code, rw- is data or stack.
Mapping tells you which file or region it is.
The last line (total kB) adds up Kbytes, RSS and Dirty for the whole process.
